+### (a) Decoder PIPE=1/STAGES=4 BAKED: **KEEP shipped** (commit `5c73b5d`) + PIPE=2 design-tournament: **REVERT** (2026-06-17)
+
+`a89f6f1` committed the deeper cp.async ring engine but left the default at PIPE=0 — the +1.49× win
+existed in source but was NOT in the shipped `_ops.so`. This commit (`5c73b5d`) bakes PIPE=1/STAGES=4
+as the default, so the win actually lands in the product binary.
+
+**Gate result (final tournament):** PIPE=2 (the producer/consumer mbarrier engine, commit `74260dd`) was
+the design-tournament challenger. Measured @ d=2048/B=16384 × 3 seeds:
+
+| Config | 3-seed mean | Roofline |
+|---|---|---|
+| PIPE=1/STAGES=4 (champion, `5c73b5d`) | **618.33 ms** | 6.477% |
+| PIPE=2 depth=2 (challenger, `74260dd`) | **857 ms** | ~4.5% |
+
+**PIPE=2 REVERT: 857 vs 618 ms, +38.6% slower on all 3 seeds. fp64+A/A/A gate passed both.**
+
+**Mechanistic WHY for PIPE=2 losing:** the producer/consumer ring splits the available threads
+between a "producer" warp-group (stages async loads) and a "consumer" warp-group (runs wgmma). The
+fwd/dX kernel is DRAIN-bound — cp.async WAIT accounts for 43% fwd / 56% dX of in-ring time. Splitting
+threads halves the producer throughput servicing the already-slow drain, so the bottleneck (the drain)
+gets more starved, not less. The PIPE=1 mechanism (simply deepening the ring from S=2 to S=4, keeping
+the full thread complement) hides drain latency by keeping more in-flight loads simultaneously without
+partitioning threads. PIPE=2 adds ring + mbarrier coordination overhead on top of a now-slower drain.
+The mechanism CONTRASTS with the P0 dW-pipeline REVERT (`Ledger:50-52`): dW was staging-bound → the
+staging bottleneck got worse when you split threads → the same failure mode.
+
+**Cumulative decoder timeline at bake:** dW-staging (1889→920 ms) × fwd/dX deeper ring (920→618 ms)
+= 1889→618 ms ≈ **3.05× from session start**; roofline 2.08% → 6.475%. Now in the shipped binary.
